**API:** `boolean allowRequest(String userId)` (pass timestamps from an injectable `Clock` for tests). Configuration: `limit X`, `window Y`.

**Algorithms**
- **Fixed window:** counter per user per window bucket (`now / Y`). O(1) and simple, but allows a burst of up to 2X across a boundary.
- **Sliding window log:** keep a deque of request timestamps per user. On each request, drop timestamps older than `now - Y`, allow if size < X, then append. Accurate; memory is O(X) per user.
- **Sliding window counter:** weight the previous and current window counts; approximate and O(1).
- **Token bucket:** tokens refill at X/Y per second up to a capacity; each request takes one. O(1) state (token count and last refill time) and it allows controlled bursts.

**Sliding window sketch**
```java
Map<String, Deque<Long>> logs = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
boolean allow(String user) {
  long now = clock.millis();
  Deque<Long> q = logs.computeIfAbsent(user, k -> new ArrayDeque<>());
  synchronized (q) {
    while (!q.isEmpty() && q.peekFirst() <= now - windowMs) q.pollFirst();
    if (q.size() < limit) { q.addLast(now); return true; }
    return false;
  }
}
```

**Thread safety:** `HashMap` is not safe for concurrent `put`; `ConcurrentHashMap` makes map operations safe. But "check size then add" is a compound action, so it needs a lock. Locking *per user* (as above) keeps different users from blocking each other, unlike a single global lock.

**Credits extension:** when a window ends with unused capacity, add a credit up to a cap (for example the unused count, max C). A request is allowed if the window has capacity, otherwise consume a credit. Keep the credit state in the same per-user object and update it inside the same lock. Define clearly whether credits expire.

**Beyond one process:** use a shared store (for example Redis with atomic scripts), and clean up idle users to avoid memory leaks.
